 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than iterative optimization methods. The method involves a single pass through a sparse 3D CNN and MLP heads, followed by recursive refinement (typically 2 iterations) for geometry convergence [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the global point cloud $\mathcal{P}$ and the feature volume $\mathcal{F}$. The sparse 3D CNN and MLP heads add model memory overhead [S2].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves real-time rendering by feed-forward prediction of Gaussian primitives [S2]. Improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods like Flash3D and DrivingForward [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles large-scale urban scenes with sparse views; decouples foreground and background modeling; reduces computation by downsampling initial primitives [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of off-the-shelf depth estimators; may require statistical filtering to handle depth noise [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than iterative optimization methods (like standard 3DGS) as it avoids per-scene gradient descent. The complexity is dominated by the sparse 3D CNN feature extraction and the MLP head evaluations [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the global point cloud $\mathcal{P}$ and the sparse feature volume $\mathcal{F}$. The downsampling of initial primitives reduces computation compared to dense pixel-aligned methods, but the global volume approach may consume more memory than local cost-volume methods [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering speeds suitable for urban scene reconstruction [S2]. It improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods like MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Global consistency, reduced ghosting artifacts, efficient feed-forward prediction, handles foreground/background separation effectively [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of monocular depth estimators; noisy depth can lead to initialization errors requiring filtering [S2]. May lose high-frequency appearance details due to downsampling, necessitating image-based rendering for color [S2].
+*   **When to use:** Urban scene reconstruction, autonomous driving applications, and scenarios requiring fast, consistent novel view synthesis from sparse inputs [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes with high overlap where iterative optimization is feasible and higher fidelity is required without the overhead of global volume construction [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the need for efficient, feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting for urban scene reconstruction, this method was introduced in EVolSplat (Miao et al., 2025) [S2]. It addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward methods that predict per-pixel aligned Gaussians using local cost volumes or epipolar transformers, which suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content [S2]. By predicting a global scene representation in a unified volume, it improves global consistency [S2]. The concept of predicting Gaussian parameters via MLP heads from a feature volume is also referenced in the context of Gaussian Representation Models (GRM) [S1].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, specifically as implemented in EVolSplat, was introduced by Sheng Miao, Jiaxin Huang, Dongfeng Bai, Xu Yan, Hongyu Zhou, Yue Wang, Bingbing Liu, Andreas Geiger, and Yiyi Liao from Zhejiang University, Huawei Noah’s Ark Lab, and the University of Tübingen [S2]. The method was published in CVPR 2025 [S2]. It addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward 3DGS methods that suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content by predicting 3D Gaussians across multiple frames within a unified volume using a 3D convolutional network [S2].
 
